arXiv:2610.17945v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: In this paper, we study the following Schr\"odinger-Newton system with a vanishing magnetic potential and an electric potential \begin{equation}\label{0.1} \left\{
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
  \begin{array}{ll}
  (-i\nabla +A(x))^2u+V(x)u=\Phi u, \ & x\in\mathbb{R}^3,\\ -\Delta \Phi =|u|^2, \ & x\in\mathbb{R}^3,
  \end{array}
  \right. \end{equation} where $i$ is the imaginary unit, $A:\mathbb{R}^3\to \mathbb{R}^3$ is a continuous magnetic potential, and $V:\mathbb{R}^3\to \mathbb{R}$ is a continuous electric potential. First, we show that the ground state energy of system with $A(x)\equiv0$ and $V(x)\equiv V_{\infty}>0$ is isolated. Using this fact together with a global compactness lemma, a barycenter function, and Brouwer degree theory, we prove that under suitable conditions on $A$ and $V$, system admits either a ground state solution or a high energy solution. Interestingly, whether a ground state solution exists is significantly affected by the magnetic potential $A$ and the electric potential $V$. Furthermore, we provide sufficient conditions that guarantee the existence of a ground state solution, as well as conditions under which no ground state solution exists and a high energy solution appears instead.

arXiv:2412.16890v3 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: In this paper, we first establish the quantitative properties for positive solutions to the Moser-Trudinger equation in the two-dimensional Poincar\'e disk $\mathbb{B}^2$: \begin{equation*}\label{mt1}
  \left\{
  \begin{aligned}
  &-\Delta_{\mathbb{B}^2}u=\lambda ue^{u^2},\ x\in\mathbb{B}^2,
  &u\to0,\ \text{when}\ \rho(x)\to\infty,
  &\|\nabla_{\mathbb{B}^2} u\|_{L^2(\mathbb{B}^2)}^2\leq M_0,
  \end{aligned}
  \right. \end{equation*} 
where $0<\lambda<\frac{1}{4}=\inf\limits_{u\in W^{1,2}(\mathbb{B}^2)\backslash\{0\}}\frac{\|\nabla_{\mathbb{B}^2}u\|_{L^2(\mathbb{B}^2)}^2}{\|u\|_{L^2(\mathbb{B}^2)}^2}$, $\rho(x)$ denotes the geodesic distance between $x$ and the origin and $M_0$ is a fixed large positive constant (see Theorem \ref{thm1}). Furthermore, by carefully analyzing the asymptotic expansion of the Dirichlet energy \(\|\nabla_{\mathbb{B}^2} u\|_{L^2(\mathbb{B}^2)}^2\) as \(\lambda \to 0\), and establishing that \[ \lim_{\substack{\lambda \to 1/4}} \int_{\mathbb{B}^2} |\nabla_{\mathbb{B}^2} u_\lambda|^2 \, dV_{\mathbb{B}^2} = +\infty \] for~the ~nonzero~least energy solution, we are able to prove the existence of a critical point for the Moser-Trudinger functional \(F(u) = \int_{\mathbb{B}^2} (e^{u^2} - 1) \, dV_{\mathbb{B}^2}\) under the constraint \(\int_{\mathbb{B}^2} |\nabla_{\mathbb{B}^2} u|^2 \, dV_{\mathbb{B}^2} = \Lambda\) for \(\Lambda \in (4\pi, +\infty)\), up to some M\"{o}bius transformation. This result is substantially different from the non-existence of critical points for the Moser-Trudinger functional in the Euclidean disk.
  Finally, by applying a local Pohozaev identity around the origin and exploiting the uniqueness of solutions to the Cauchy initial value problem for ODEs, we prove that the Moser-Trudinger equation has a unique positive solution up to some M\"{o}bius transformation when $\lambda$ is close to $0$.

arXiv:2505.19021v2 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: For $n\geqslant3$ and $0<\alpha<n$, we study positive classical solutions of critical Hartree equations that blow up at the origin. For the global problem on $\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\}$, with the Hartree potential integrated over $\mathbb R^n$, we prove that solutions in $L^p(\mathbb R^n)$, where $p=(n+\alpha)/(n-2)$, are radial and nonincreasing in $|x|$. We establish an integral representation and apply moving spheres to this nonlocal problem. For the local problem on $B_R\setminus\{0\}$, with the Hartree potential integrated over $B_R$, we prove $u(x)\leqslant C|x|^{-(n-2)/2}$ near the origin and \begin{equation*}
  u(x)=\bar u(|x|)\bigl(1+\mathcal O(|x|)\bigr)\qquad\text{as }x\to0, \end{equation*} where $\bar u$ is the spherical average of $u$. A main difficulty is controlling the nonlocal Hartree potential under Kelvin inversion and in the blow-up limit. The local proof combines blow-up analysis and moving spheres with estimates of the integral tails and an auxiliary Liouville lemma. We also give an alternative proof of the classification of positive $C^2$ solutions of the critical Hartree equation in $\mathbb R^n$.
